# offhand-swivel.xrs - does the OFF hand move when only the HELD hand rotates?
#
# The reported bug (s71): "Rotating the right hand causes the left one to move as
# well... it doesnt apply rotation to the left hand, but it just moves it
# positionally in a swivel depending on right hand rotation horizontally."
#
# THIS IS THE TEST A HUMAN CANNOT RUN. The whole question is whether the off hand
# moves *while its own controller is still*, and a person cannot hold a
# controller still to the precision the answer needs - every headset run of this
# was confounded by the tester's own off-hand drift. The simulator parks the left
# controller on an exact pose and never touches it again, so anything the left
# hand does afterwards was caused by the RIGHT hand and nothing else.
#
# THE ORACLE IS A PAIR, and neither half is sufficient alone:
#
# 1. THE INPUT, from the mod's own log - FREETARGET prints the world point the
#    free hand is ASKED to reach, beside the held hand's rotator:
#      grep -a FREETARGET %LOCALAPPDATA%\BioshockVR\bioshockvr.log
#    Across this sweep that point must not change. Measured 2026-08-30: it was
#    BIT-IDENTICAL - (-37343.1 491.4 7801.2) at held yaw -18.2, -27.1 and -146.2.
#
# 2. THE OUTPUT, from the captures:
#      .\tools\img-diff.ps1 -A swivel_yawP60_left.png -B swivel_yawM60_left.png -Grid 12
#    The off hand occupies the lower-CENTRE-LEFT of the frame. If that block
#    lights up while its controller never moved, the bug is live.
#
# Input identical + output moved = the defect is downstream of the target
# computation. That pairing is the finding; run BOTH halves or the run is
# worthless. Measured 2026-08-30 in Medical Pavilion: the off hand was visible at
# lower-centre-left at yaw -60 and absent at yaw +60, with the input identical.
#
# READ THE BBOX, NOT THE MEAN. BS1 interiors are dark and a hand crossing the
# frame can read below the standing-still floor on mean-abs-diff - the trap
# coupling-hand.xrs already documents.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it cannot load a save. Run it against a level where a
# weapon is already equipped and both hands are drawn; an empty-handed or
# menu-state run answers nothing, because there is no off hand to move.
#
# Stereo is armed here rather than left to the caller, for the reason
# coupling-hand.xrs gives: `vrstereo on` is sticky and testing mono has misled
# this project before.
# ================== PRECONDITION. DO NOT SKIP. ==================
#
# THE SIM HOLDING A POSE DOES NOT MEAN THE MOD IS READING IT. Measured
# 2026-08-30, and it invalidated a whole run that had already been reported as
# a reproduction: xrsim-state showed handR exactly where it was put
# (pos -0.60 1.30 -0.45) while the mod's own FREETARGET line showed the held
# actor FROZEN at yaw -27.1, loc (-37374.7 449.1 7868.8) through a 1.8 m
# controller move. The captures still differed - because BS1's scenery,
# lighting and NPCs move on their own - so the diff looked exactly like a
# reproduction and was not one.
#
# So before believing ANY capture from this file, prove the plumbing:
#
#   .\tools\xrsim-cmd.ps1 "hand r grip pose  1.20 1.30 -0.45 0 0 0"
#   .\tools\xrsim-cmd.ps1 "hand r grip pose -0.60 1.30 -0.45 0 0 0"
#   grep -a FREETARGET %LOCALAPPDATA%\BioshockVR\bioshockvr.log | tail -3
#
# The `actorLoc=` field MUST move by roughly 100 UU per metre (coupling-hand.xrs
# records 110 UU for 1.1 m). If it does not move, the mod is not seeing the
# simulated controllers and every capture below is a picture of the scenery.
# ================================================================
reset
@mod vrstereo on
@mod vrhands on; vrhands mode brvr
@frames 60
@assert projectionViews eq 2

# THE OFF HAND IS PARKED HERE AND NEVER TOUCHED AGAIN. Every `hand` line below
# addresses `r` only. If you add a `hand l` line after this point you have
# destroyed the experiment.
hand l grip pose -0.25 1.30 -0.40 0 0 0
hand r grip pose 0.25 1.30 -0.45 0 0 0
@frames 40
@shot swivel_yaw00
@assert layersLastFrame ge 3

# Sweep the HELD hand's yaw only - position and pitch/roll held constant, so yaw
# is the single variable. +60 and -60 bracket the reported "horizontally".
hand r grip pose 0.25 1.30 -0.45 0 60 0
@frames 40
@shot swivel_yawP60

hand r grip pose 0.25 1.30 -0.45 0 -60 0
@frames 40
@shot swivel_yawM60

# Back to centre. A hand that does NOT return to its yaw00 position is a second
# finding - it would mean the coupling is cumulative rather than a function of
# the current pose, which is a different bug with a different fix.
hand r grip pose 0.25 1.30 -0.45 0 0 0
@frames 40
@shot swivel_yaw00_return
